00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.180
Self-driving cars are coming.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.760
Maybe in two years, maybe in twenty,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.110
But someday the steering wheel will fade into
history like Jell-O Salad and these weird

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:19.110
coasters.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.360
The elderly and disabled will have cheap,
convenient transportation,

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.720
Instead of racing to work, you’ll sit back,
relax, and generate ad revenue for me.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.239
and crashes will go from unfortunate reality
to rare tragedy.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:38.319
When three technologies converge: self-driving,
electric, and shared cars,

00:00:38.319 --> 00:00:43.430
the problem of how to get from A to B will
pretty much be solved.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:48.920
For the 68% of us living in cities, there
will be no need to own a car.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.229
In this world, transportation is a service,
waiting on every street, corner, nook, and

00:00:54.229 --> 00:00:55.229
alley.

00:00:55.229 --> 00:00:57.199
No waiting, hailing, or repairing.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:00:59.649
Of course, convenience is nothing new.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:02.299
The real force of progress is economic.

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:05.920
Without the cost of drivers and gasoline,
prices fall dramatically.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.049
They won’t just be faster, easier, safer,
and, ya, know, better for the planet or whatever,

00:01:12.049 --> 00:01:14.460
but also affordable for everyone.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.009
The car will become the ideal form of transport.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:21.600
Not despite its cost, but, in part, because
of it.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.880
and that’s the problem: More cars means
more congestion, more parking, more challenges

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:27.940
for cities.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.740
You might be thinking, sure, and automation
can solve them.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:32.740
Perhaps.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.590
But mass transportation is a very complicated
and political equation.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.600
It’s not that self-driving cars are bad,
exactly the opposite, it’s that so much

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.399
optimism hides real obstacles, or, at the
very least, question marks, on the road to

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.760
the city of the future.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.460
Like many technologies, individually, self-driving cars are great

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.080
Collectively, they have all kinds of unexpected
consequences.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:03.320
Everything about them we hear through the
lens of technology, and when you’re a programmer,

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:05.680
everything looks like Ruby on rails.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.970
To really understand them, we need the mindset
of a Sim City professional.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:15.550
And at the top of things Urban Planners hate
are: highways, parking lots, wide roads, and

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:16.550
suburbs.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.020
Basically… cars.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.350
Or everything America is obsessed with.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:25.660
Today, electric vehicles make up less than
one percent of new car sales in the U.S.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.860
Even in Norway, where it’s 22%, that’s
only 84,000 electric, compared to one billion

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:32.860
total.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:37.220
In other words, we’re a long ways away from
most cars being electric.

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:43.420
Until then, they release 13% of all greenhouse
gas, kill more people from pollution than

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:49.150
crashes, and make cities look and sound like…
well, this.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:54.960
But even if every car were electric tomorrow,
and self-driving technology was perfect, AND

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.840
everyone could afford it, there’s still
a fundamental problem: space, the most valuable

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.060
resource in a dense, urban area.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.690
60 people commuting to work can look like
this, or it can look like this.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:11.820
If we’re generous and assume two people
per car, that’s still not 60.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.890
Imagine how wasteful sidewalks would be if
everyone required a 2-meter buffer.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.400
Well, you don’t have to.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.460
Fine, you say, so we’ll build more and bigger
roads.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.890
Do not pass go, and do not collect two hundred
dollars.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:30.260
This is a classic mistake, because bigger
roads do work, just, only for a minute.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.250
As roads improve, more people drive, until
it’s just as busy as it was before.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.250
Bigger roads?

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:37.250
More traffic.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.250
Same congestion.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.620
Remember: transportation is an ecosystem.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.950
You can pull any lever you want, and with
the best intentions, but change one variable,

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.430
and three dominoes down the line, who knows
what happens.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.450
Trust me, I watch levers get pulled all the
time.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:57.470
Plus, money spent on roads isn’t spent improving
trains, bikes, and busses.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.910
So, why do roads get bigger anyway?

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:03.060
In part, because politicians serve short terms.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.800
Reduce commute time, get reelected, and then
watch it return to normal.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.900
Roads determine the shape, health, and success
of a city,

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.610
they push buildings further apart, and housing
into suburbs.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:20.450
These long distances between home and work,
or school, or shopping, encourage, and often

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:21.910
require, the use of a car.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:27.950
What could be an easily walked or biked community,
is stretched out into an endless sea of buildings

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:32.880
where people get less exercise, make fewer
friends, even spend less money.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.230
But for 92% of their life, cars just sit.

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:42.150
In the U.S. alone, there are one billion parking
spaces, four times the number of cars, or,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.210
together, the size of Connecticut.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:49.530
Most cities have Parking Minimums, For example,
a bowling alley in San Jose, California must

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:52.750
provide at least 7 parking spaces per bowling
lane.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:59.780
A business office requires 1 for every 250
square feet, but a research office, only 300.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.470
Which is just as arbitrary as it sounds.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.900
In 2004, the city of London removed these
minimums, and parking fell from 1.1 spaces

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.280
per apartment to 0.6.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:15.190
And since businesses are incentivized to create
as much parking as customers need, this switch

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.500
from free to free-market parking shows there
was previously too much.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.380
Of course, parking is never really free, In
residential areas, it hides in the cost of

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.380
rent.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.250
In the U.S., that’s an average of $225 a
month, sometimes much more.

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:34.110
And taxpayers bear the cost of free public
parking whether they use it every day or can’t

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.220
afford a car and only breathe-in their fumes.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:42.070
Worse, Americans incentivize driving with
the federal Commuter Tax Benefit,

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:46.170
7.3 Billion dollars a year in write-offs for
those who drive to work.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:51.590
This alone causes an estimated 4.6 billion
more miles to be driven, and moves wealth

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.230
from poor to rich.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:57.520
Meanwhile, the government loses 7 billion
dollars, Which, I have a feeling it could

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.900
find a better use for…

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.340
Now, you might be thinking, all this talk
of parking and sprawl and roads, it assumes

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.110
more cars in the future.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:08.070
But when we share them, won’t there actually
be fewer?

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.300
Well, maybe.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.270
But there’s good reason to be skeptical.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:16.620
For every mile shared Uber and Lyft rides
save, they add 2.6.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:17.620
How is that possible?

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:22.050
45% of the time they’re empty, driving to
and from pickups.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:27.080
and 60% of riders say they would’ve otherwise
walked, or biked, or stayed home.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:31.650
Driverless cars will be more efficient, but
not fundamentally different, there’ll be

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:37.169
empty, wasted miles, and many who share will
do so instead of a more efficient means of

00:06:37.169 --> 00:06:38.169
transportation.

00:06:38.169 --> 00:06:41.180
And because demand fluctuates, they’ll still
need parking.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:45.669
Having enough cars to get everyone to and
from work leaves many with nothing to do for

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:46.669
the rest of the day.

00:06:46.669 --> 00:06:50.810
They could go park outside the city, but that
means more miles driven.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:54.220
Plus, they all need somewhere for recharging,
cleaning, and maintenance.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.430
Now, here’s the thing: It’s easy to imagine
a future where automation is so efficient,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.180
none of this really matters,

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:07.730
For example, this study estimates a 25-35%
increase in traffic flow, which is very encouraging,

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:12.199
Here’s the catch: only when nearly every
vehicle is self-driving.

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:16.750
Eventually, cars will no longer have the monkey
coordination problem, where one car can slow

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.010
down a city.

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:22.090
But that’s the key: It only takes a single
non-self-driving car.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:26.220
Even with 75% driverless, the study says “the
improvements are minor.”

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.650
So, someday they may solve the transportation
problem, but first, they could make it much

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.840
worse.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:43.180
The most efficient way to travel isn’t the car, or train, or plane, it’s… your feet - minimal infrastructure, and a very small well… footprint

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.680
Almost as good are bicycles and skateboards.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:50.199
Of course, these alone are not enough, so
transportation is a balance.

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:54.680
It’s not bus versus rail versus car, but
all of the above.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:59.620
When twice a day, 5 days a week, thousands
of people need to get from roughly here to

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.820
roughly here, well, you’re probably looking
for rapid transit.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:08.810
Within the city, where routes and demand change
over time, the bus is probably your answer.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.340
But self-driving cars threaten this balance.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.090
The first to adopt them will be those that
can afford it,

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:18.520
And as more and more upper and middle class
disappear from public transit, so will its

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:19.520
funding disappear.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.680
To them, public transit will seem old and
unnecessary.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:26.479
But it’ll still be very important for much
of the population.

00:08:26.479 --> 00:08:30.729
If cities want to keep an efficient balance,
they need to prioritize accordingly

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:36.379
Designing first for walking and biking - open
spaces, clean sidewalks, and instead of fighting

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:39.259
the homeless, well, maybe fighting homelessness.

00:08:39.259 --> 00:08:43.699
After that, encouraging public transit - the
most efficient, cost-effective way to move

00:08:43.699 --> 00:08:45.160
huge groups of people.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.699
And finally, controlling the number of cars,

00:08:47.699 --> 00:08:52.620
Making sure drivers themselves pay their costs,
including those you can’t see.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.629
The price of tolls and parking should increase
with demand, creating an incentive not to

00:08:57.629 --> 00:09:00.480
drive, and managing congestion during peak
hours.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.470
None of this will be easy, but, ultimately,
transportation is an equation.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:09.470
The difference between an overcrowded city
and a comfortable one is finding the optimal,

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.180
mathematical solution.

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:15.790
Cities have to find the right balance of old
technologies and new ones like dock-less,

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.740
electric scooters, bike sharing, even Segways.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:23.869
